      "id": "content.act1.news.launch",
      "appSurface": "news",
      "actAvailable": 1,
      "order": 0,
      "initialVersionID": "version.act1.news.launch.v1",
      "versions": [
        {
          "id": "version.act1.news.launch.v1",
          "version": 1,
          "title": "Banana Vision launches tomorrow. What should we expect?",
          "subtitle": "A familiar company is betting on a very different kind of screen.",
          "author": "Circuit Daily",
          "source": "Circuit Daily",
          "timestampLabel": "Today · 09:20",
          "narrativeTimestamp": {
            "day": 1,
            "hour": 9,
            "minute": 20
          },
          "payload": {
            "type": "news",
            "data": {
              "publication": "Circuit Daily",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Banana Corp will introduce Banana Vision tomorrow, putting its mixed-reality ambitions in front of a much larger audience. The company describes the AR/VR headset as a way to place digital tools alongside the world rather than behind another rectangle."
                },
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The demonstrations emphasize ordinary tasks: arranging a workspace, sharing a large viewing surface and moving between digital and physical surroundings. Banana Corp says its adaptive interface will make those transitions feel natural. Independent launch coverage will need to test how well that promise holds up outside a demonstration."
                },
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "For now, the message is unmistakably ambitious: “A New Way to See Everything.” Tomorrow’s presentation should offer the first clear look at whether the hardware can make that slogan practical."
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          "effectsOnRead": [
            {
              "type": "markMilestone",
              "id": "milestone.act1.news_context"
            }
          ],
          "preservedEvidenceID": "evidence.act1.news.launch.v1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "content.act1.news.interface",
      "appSurface": "news",
      "actAvailable": 1,
      "order": 10,
      "initialVersionID": "version.act1.news.interface.v1",
      "versions": [
        {
          "id": "version.act1.news.interface.v1",
          "version": 1,
          "title": "The interface is the real Banana Vision pitch",
          "subtitle": "The headset promises less menu navigation, not just more screen space.",
          "author": "Interface Review",
          "source": "Interface Review",
          "timestampLabel": "Today · 09:21",
          "narrativeTimestamp": {
            "day": 1,
            "hour": 9,
            "minute": 21
          },
          "payload": {
            "type": "news",
            "data": {
              "publication": "Interface Review",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Headset announcements often begin with display specifications. Banana Corp has taken a different approach, showing people bringing useful information into view and letting it recede when they turn back to their surroundings."
                },
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Its public materials call this an adaptive interface: presentation that responds to the person using it. During a preview briefing, the company said its internal review checks whether adaptations remain consistent with a user’s selected preferences. That distinction may matter more than any single feature on the launch slide."
                },
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "The demos are carefully chosen, and a demo is not a long-term test. We will be looking for clear settings and predictable behavior when Banana Vision launches tomorrow. An interface that gets out of the way still needs to explain what it is doing."
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          "effectsOnRead": [
            {
              "type": "markMilestone",
              "id": "milestone.act1.news_context"
            }
          ],
          "preservedEvidenceID": "evidence.act1.news.interface.v1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "content.act1.news.influence",
      "appSurface": "news",
      "actAvailable": 1,
      "order": 20,
      "initialVersionID": "version.act1.news.influence.v1",
      "versions": [
        {
          "id": "version.act1.news.influence.v1",
          "version": 1,
          "title": "Why tomorrow’s headset launch matters beyond Banana Corp",
          "subtitle": "Developers and competitors are watching the same announcement.",
          "author": "Market & Matter",
          "source": "Market & Matter",
          "timestampLabel": "Today · 09:22",
          "narrativeTimestamp": {
            "day": 1,
            "hour": 9,
            "minute": 22
          },
          "payload": {
            "type": "news",
            "data": {
              "publication": "Market & Matter",
              "blocks": [
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Banana Corp already occupies a familiar place in personal technology. Banana Vision is its attempt to make that influence extend into the space around a device, rather than stop at its screen."
                },
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Developers are watching the company’s launch materials for a practical answer to a simple question: what will people choose to do in mixed reality every day? Large work surfaces and shared entertainment are promising examples, but the broader ecosystem will depend on useful experiences people return to."
                },
                {
                  "kind": "paragraph",
                  "text": "Tomorrow’s launch has become a talking point well beyond headset enthusiasts. It is a test of how much room a major technology company can open for a category that still needs to earn a place in ordinary routines."
                }
              ]
            }
          },
          "effectsOnRead": [
            {
              "type": "markMilestone",
              "id": "milestone.act1.news_context"
            }
          ],
          "preservedEvidenceID": "evidence.act1.news.influence.v1"
        }
      ]
    },
